---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/roofing';
import { roofMaterials, UNDERLAYMENTS, NAILS_PER_SQUARE, RIDGE_CAP_LF_PER_BUNDLE, STARTER_LF_PER_BUNDLE } from '../lib/roof';
import { pitchFactor } from '../lib/finish';
import { fmt, int } from '../lib/format';

const base = { pitch: 6, wastePct: 10, bundlesPerSquare: 3, underlayCoverFt2: UNDERLAYMENTS[0].coverFt2 };
const ex = roofMaterials({ ...base, lengthFt: 50, widthFt: 30, type: 'gable' });
const hip = roofMaterials({ ...base, lengthFt: 50, widthFt: 30, type: 'hip' });
const houses = [
  { name: '1,000 sq ft ranch (40 × 25 ft)', l: 40, w: 25 },
  { name: '1,500 sq ft (50 × 30 ft)', l: 50, w: 30 },
  { name: '2,000 sq ft (50 × 40 ft)', l: 50, w: 40 },
  { name: '2,500 sq ft (62.5 × 40 ft)', l: 62.5, w: 40 },
].map((h) => ({ ...h, r4: roofMaterials({ ...base, pitch: 4, lengthFt: h.l, widthFt: h.w, type: 'gable' }), r6: roofMaterials({ ...base, lengthFt: h.l, widthFt: h.w, type: 'gable' }), r8: roofMaterials({ ...base, pitch: 8, lengthFt: h.l, widthFt: h.w, type: 'gable' }) }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="roofing-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Roofing Calculator: Roof Squares & Full Material List"
  description="Free roofing calculator. Enter the roof footprint, pitch, and shape to get the roof area in squares and a full list: shingles, underlayment, drip edge, starter, ridge cap, and nails."
  h1="Roofing Calculator"
  updated="2026-10-24"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Measure your roof from the ground and get its size in squares plus everything on the material list, from shingle
    bundles to drip edge and ridge cap. Add your roofer's price per square to check a quote.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to measure a roof</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure the length and width of the house from outside, then add the overhangs on each side. This is the footprint.</li>
    <li>Find the pitch (rise per 12 inches of run). The <a href="/roof-pitch-calculator/">roof pitch calculator</a> shows three ways to measure it.</li>
    <li>Multiply the footprint by the pitch factor for the sloped roof area.</li>
    <li>Add 10% for a gable roof or 15% for hips, valleys, and dormers, then divide by 100 for <strong>squares</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 50 × 30 ft gable roof at 6/12</h2>
  <p>
    The footprint is 1,500 square feet. At 6/12 the pitch factor is {fmt(pitchFactor(6), 3)}, so the roof is
    {fmt(ex.sloped, 0)} square feet. With 10% waste that is <strong>{fmt(ex.squares, 1)} squares</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>{ex.bundles} bundles of shingles at 3 per square</li>
    <li>{ex.underlayRolls} rolls of synthetic underlayment</li>
    <li>{ex.dripEdgePieces} pieces of drip edge for {fmt(ex.dripEdgeFt, 0)} ft of eaves and rakes</li>
    <li>{ex.starterBundles} bundle of starter strip and {ex.ridgeCapBundles} bundles of ridge cap</li>
    <li>about {int(ex.nails)} roofing nails</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The same house with a hip roof has {fmt(hip.eavesFt, 0)} ft of eaves and {fmt(hip.ridgeCapFt, 0)} ft of ridge and hips,
    so it needs {hip.starterBundles} bundles of starter and {hip.ridgeCapBundles} of ridge cap.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Roofing squares by house size</h2>
  <p>Gable roof, footprint including overhangs, 10% waste.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>House</th><th class="num">4/12</th><th class="num">6/12</th><th class="num">8/12</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{houses.map((h) => <tr><td>{h.name}</td><td class="num">{fmt(h.r4.squares, 1)} sq</td><td class="num">{fmt(h.r6.squares, 1)} sq</td><td class="num">{fmt(h.r8.squares, 1)} sq</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>A house's living area and its footprint are not the same: a two-story home has a much smaller roof than its square footage suggests.</p>

  <h2 id="cost">Checking a roofing quote</h2>
  <p>
    Roofers price by the square installed. Multiply the squares from this calculator by the price per square on your quote
    to check that the measurement matches. Tear-off of old layers, disposal, replacing rotten decking, and extra flashing
    are often billed separately, so ask what the square price includes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="materials">What goes on the material list</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>How it is counted</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Shingles</td><td>3 bundles per square for most shingles, 4 for some heavy ones</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Underlayment</td><td>By sloped area: synthetic about 10 squares per roll, #15 felt 4, #30 felt 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Along every eave and rake, in 10 ft pieces</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starter strip</td><td>Along the eaves, about {STARTER_LF_PER_BUNDLE} ft per bundle</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ridge cap</td><td>Along ridges and hips, about {RIDGE_CAP_LF_PER_BUNDLE} ft per bundle</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nails</td><td>About {NAILS_PER_SQUARE} per square with four nails per shingle; six-nail patterns need half again as many</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Coverage per bundle and roll varies by brand. The wrapper states the exact number.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many square feet is a roofing square?</h3>
  <p>One square is 100 square feet of roof surface, measured along the slope.</p>
  <h3>How do I measure my roof without climbing on it?</h3>
  <p>
    Measure the outside of the house at ground level, add the overhangs, and use the pitch factor. That gives the sloped
    area to within a few percent for simple gable and hip roofs.
  </p>
  <h3>Does a steeper roof need more shingles?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. The same footprint has more surface at a steeper pitch: a 12/12 roof has about 41% more area than its footprint,
    a 4/12 roof about 5% more.
  </p>
  <p>Only need the shingle count? The <a href="/shingle-calculator/">shingle calculator</a> gives bundles by pitch.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sloped area = footprint × √(1 + (pitch ÷ 12)²); squares = area × (1 + waste %) ÷ 100.</li>
    <li>Gable: eaves = 2 × the long side, rakes = 4 × half the short side × pitch factor, ridge = the long side.</li>
    <li>Hip: eaves = the full perimeter, ridge = long side − short side, each hip = √(2 × (half width)² + rise²).</li>
    <li>The roof is assumed to have one pitch throughout. Underlayment is counted on the area before waste; all counts round up.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
